i admire neo the world ends with you for  its youthful attitude through eccentric  personalities and character designs  and it's so cheesy it's good vibe it  brings back how i felt all those years  ago with its predecessor  but that's also because while a sequel  that can be enjoyed on its own  its adherence to the original story of  the world ends with you brought me back  to another time  which might leave you lost if it passed  you by still  neo tuiwi has its share of attractions  like a standout action rpg combat system  that evolves into an exciting rush of  flashy spells filling up the screen  and while you might roll your eyes at  the cast of characters quirks in the  beginning  they'll grow on you like good friends  who are annoying at first the same can  be said about its soundtrack  songs that are odd upon first listen  become bops that get stuck in your head  [Music]  this is also a story heavy rpg with  intriguing twists and turns  however in its shallow riddle filled  objectives  and narrative wheel spinning  neo-twee-wee drags its feet for a bit  too long and too often before reaching  its payoff  now the setup is that the reapers game  is back a parallel dimension of real  world japan  characters trapped in it have been  posthumously invited to play a game of  ambiguous rules and objectives  for another chance at life but rules are  meant to be broken and parameters are  meant to be manipulated  so much so that you eventually disregard  its logic and just embrace the cool  nonsense  used to bend the fate of the characters  and the setting of shibuya itself  main protagonist rindo and his friends  fret and nagi all teenagers who  consistently rack their brains to  overcome the reaper's game's seemingly  impossible odds  they drive the desperate fight for  survival and supporting characters  whether they're friend or foe  make the core ensemble all the more  stronger while rindo is your avatar and  the level-headed empath  most of the supporting characters fill  in archetypes with confidence and their  own personal touch  which is also a testament to the great  performances by the voice cast  we've got this in the  [Music]  not bag you're just doing my job  characters are at the heart of neo-tuiwi  from their striking fashion sense  to their overstated idiosyncrasies it's  how you come to understand them  and they are what makes this game  memorable there are some notable misses  and unlikable characters but the wide  cast and the freedom of expression  neotiwie affords these personalities  make it easy to find who you identify  with and it's one of the strongest hooks  here  i definitely can't get into spoilers but  those who love the original tui  including myself  are treated to some hype moments through  important story connections and sweet  callbacks  a few aspects of this do come across as  ham fisted  but i also can't deny the jolt of  nostalgia that made the experience all  the more enjoyable  ultimately that leaves those who never  engaged with the original twee-wee  out of the loop nyotiwi tries to  accommodate by throwing in vague  references to what happened in the first  game  so neo-tuiwi will hit differently for  different folks  as for the storytelling neo-tuiwi takes  a long time to get the ball rolling  and sometimes drags its feet even when  things start to really heat up  it's not that the exposition is bad it's  actually where a ton of the charm comes  from  letting characters express themselves  and imprint their mannerisms in your  mind  it's just that there's so much of it and  it often feels like filler  and dialogue for the sake of dialogue  there is a lot to work through and not  all of it is particularly fascinating  it hurts its momentum by dragging you  through wild goose chases  funneling you from point to point for a  chunk of dialogue to move the story just  a step  forward and you're frequently asked to  solve riddles or investigate anomaly  which can sometimes be an interesting  brand exercise  but these moments frequently feel like  busy work rather than actually engaging  with the game's world  neo-tweewy is backloaded with some of  its best moments  but runs the risk of losing your  interest before it really gets spicy or  starts to pay off  it's also worth noting that most of the  game's story is told through character  portraits and panels  perhaps  exactly the game's wonderful art style  complements the approach  but the over-reliance on character  stills takes away from feeling present  in a bustling city like shibuya and that  things are actually happening in those  streets  it's a shame too because shibuya is  vibrant and the game's few cutscenes are  stunning  unfortunately in several story critical  moments you have to use quite a bit of  your imagination to visualize what's  happening  instead of actually seeing it unfold  combat picks up some of that slack  though  the chaos of the action-packed battles  is a visual treat especially as things  ramp up when extra party members join  the fight and thankfully it's a blast to  play too  you're done  so far  the foundation of combat are pins which  are equippable spells and attacks that  get mapped to a specific button  for a certain character depending on the  attack type you have melee range  charge damage over time and area of  effect abilities all of which can be  fired off simultaneously  or strategically used to string together  combos with good timing  doing so is rewarded in boosting your  groove meter which then lets you cast a  powerful  super-like ability to further pound your  enemies  you're constantly earning new pins  throughout the game giving you new ways  to engage in combat  and rethink what's most effective for  your party once you start building up a  collection of pins  devising effective combos and reading  the patterns of the various enemy types  neo-tweewy's gameplay really shines it  becomes absolute chaos when you start  stacking and juggling multiple attacks  yet remains fast paced and satisfying  what's more is that combat difficulty  can be fine-tuned at any moment in your  journey  neo twee-wee features the basic easy  normal and hard  or extra hard if you unlock it but also  lets you slide the party's level down in  increments  to further tweak the way you're  challenged it's also a means for  adjusting how you earn rewards like new  pins and experience points  in the overworld you can choose when you  engage with enemies and you can stack  encounters before initiating them you  could also just worry about the battles  necessary to advance a story if you want  neo-tuiwi is designed to accommodate  various play styles in creative ways  shibuya as your overworld is one of the  joys of neo-tuiwi as well  shopping for new gear and ordering food  to boost your stats in the different  districts  is a nice way to connect to the city and  build up your party  side quests while fairly basic in design  represent the lives of the people who  fill the streets  these help you unlock some in-game perks  and a few of these have neat little  stories to boot  lastly one of the most significant  pieces in creating the distinct  atmosphere  is the oddball yet lovable soundtrack  with a complete disregard for consistent  music genre you'll hear  grungy punk or full-on hardcore tunes  one minute then some catchy j-pop and  j-rock style bops  before going into up-tempo synthwave  [Music]  and overall it's so good music was  paramount to the first game's identity  and neo-tuiwi brings the heat again it's  a big way the game doubles down on its  attitude  and you can't help but admire that  despite my gripes with the ways nyotuiwi  almost stunts my enthusiasm for it  i'm glad it exists i can live with the  lulls along the way for its memorable  moments  and i can still wish it did more with  storytelling while loving its  personalities  sense of style and connections to the  original it's largely made for those  with a fondness for the first game  which becomes increasingly apparent as  the story goes on so your mileage may  vary  but the world of tui is a unique one  that offers a chance to feel  young wild and free and it's packaged as  a frenetic rpg that can be cheesy as  hell  in both its serious and lighthearted  moments but after embracing that it's a  journey worth considering
 

